---
import ServicePage from "../../components/ServicePage.astro";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "How much does tree removal cost in South Florida?",
    a: `It depends on the size of the tree, how close it is to the house, power lines or a pool, and how much access there is for equipment. A small tree in an open yard costs far less than a large oak over a roof that needs climbing or a crane. Our <a href="/tree-care-guides/tree-removal-cost-south-florida/">cost guide</a> explains what drives the price, and the estimate you receive will be for your specific tree.`,
  },
  {
    q: "Who actually removes the tree?",
    a: `An independent tree care professional, matched to your request. SF Tree Removal is an online service that passes your request to them; we do not perform the work. You agree the scope, price and schedule directly with the professional, and we recommend asking for proof of license and insurance before work starts.`,
  },
  {
    q: "Do I need a permit to remove a tree?",
    a: `Often yes, but it depends on your city, the type and size of the tree, and whether it is dangerous. Florida law exempts dangerous trees on residential property when a certified arborist documents the risk. See our <a href="/tree-removal-permits/">permit guide</a>.`,
  },
  {
    q: "Is stump grinding and cleanup included?",
    a: `It varies between professionals, so ask for it to be written in the estimate: whether the wood and branches are hauled away, and whether the stump is ground below ground level. Both are normally quoted together with the removal.`,
  },
  {
    q: "Is there free tree removal?",
    a: `The estimate is free, the removal usually is not. See <a href="/free-tree-removal/">what is actually free and how to pay less</a>.`,
  },
  {
    q: "How quickly will someone contact me?",
    a: `We pass requests on as quickly as we can. If a tree is on a house, car or driveway, choose Emergency Tree Removal in the form so the request is flagged as urgent.`,
  },
];
const extras = [
  {
    "topper": "What to expect",
    "title": "What Happens on Removal Day",
    "image": "ladder-oak",
    "html": "<p>A professional crew works to a plan, not just a chainsaw. Typically the day goes like this:</p><ol><li><strong>Walk-through.</strong> The crew checks the tree, what it could hit, access for equipment and where each piece will land.</li><li><strong>Protecting the property.</strong> Lawns, pavers, pool decks and fences are protected, and people and pets are kept clear of the work zone.</li><li><strong>Rigging and lowering.</strong> Large limbs are roped and lowered in sections, or lifted out with a crane or lift, so nothing drops on a roof or a wall.</li><li><strong>Felling the trunk.</strong> Once the crown is down, the trunk is cut in sections or felled into a cleared area.</li><li><strong>Cleanup.</strong> Wood and branches are cut up and hauled away unless you agreed to keep firewood or mulch, and the yard is raked.</li><li><strong>Stump.</strong> Grinding is usually done in the same visit if you asked for it.</li></ol>"
  },
  {
    "topper": "Difficult jobs",
    "title": "Large Trees, Cranes and Tight Spaces",
    "image": "aerial-lift",
    "html": "<p>Some removals cannot be done with a climbing crew alone. Trees over a house, next to a pool enclosure or in a back yard with no truck access may need a <strong>crane or an aerial lift</strong>. That costs more, but is often the safest and cheapest way to avoid damage.</p><p>Tell the professional about gates, narrow side yards, screen enclosures, pools and overhead lines when you send the request. Trees near power lines are handled by the utility, in most of South Florida FPL, and you should never cut them yourself.</p><p>Photos from two or three angles, with something for scale, help a professional give you a closer estimate before anyone visits.</p>"
  },
  {
    "topper": "Decision guide",
    "title": "When to Remove and When to Prune",
    "image": "arborist-inspect",
    "html": "<p>Not every unhealthy-looking tree has to come down. As a rule of thumb:</p><ul><li><strong>Remove</strong> when the tree is dead, has a split or cracked trunk, is leaning with lifted roots, is badly decayed at the base, or its roots are damaging the foundation or pipes.</li><li><strong>Prune</strong> when the tree is healthy but crowds the roof, has some dead limbs or needs thinning for storm season.</li><li><strong>Get an assessment</strong> when you are not sure. See <a href=\"/certified-arborist/\">arborist assessments</a> and our guide to the <a href=\"/tree-care-guides/signs-a-tree-needs-to-be-removed/\">warning signs a tree needs to be removed</a>.</li></ul>"
  }
];
---

<ServicePage
  title="Tree Removal in South Florida | Free Local Estimates"
  description="Get free estimates for tree removal in South Florida from independent local pros: large, dead and storm-damaged trees, palms and stumps."
  service={{
    name: "Tree Removal Estimates",
    serviceType: "Tree removal estimate matching",
    description: "Free estimates for tree removal from independent local tree care professionals in South Florida.",
  }}
  badge="TREE REMOVAL"
  h1="Tree Removal in South Florida"
  lead="Tell us about the tree and get a free, no-obligation estimate from an independent local tree care professional."
  intro={[
    "Removing a tree safely is a specialist job. Large trees near a house, pool, fence or power line need rigging, climbing or a crane, and one mistake can cost far more than the removal itself.",
    "SF Tree Removal saves you the searching. Send one request and we connect you with an independent tree care professional who gives you a free estimate. We are active in the Fort Lauderdale area and adding professionals elsewhere; see <a href=\"/areas/#coverage\">where we are active</a>. We don't do the work ourselves, so you can compare the quote and settle every detail directly with the professional.",
  ]}
  coversTitle="Trees You Can Get an Estimate For"
  covers={[
    { name: "Large and mature trees", note: "Oaks, mahogany, ficus and other big trees close to roofs, driveways and neighbors, where the removal has to be planned piece by piece." },
    { name: "Dead, dying and leaning trees", note: "Trees with a brown canopy, large dead limbs, a sudden lean or cracks in the trunk, before the next storm decides for you." },
    { name: "Storm-damaged trees", note: "Split trunks, hanging limbs and uprooted trees after tropical storms. For anything on a structure, use the emergency form." },
    { name: "Palm trees", note: "Royal, coconut and other tall palms next to pools, houses and pavers, where the crown has to be lowered carefully." },
    { name: "Trees near power lines", note: "Work close to lines needs the utility or specialist crews. Do not touch or cut anything in contact with a line." },
    { name: "Stumps", note: "Grinding below ground level so you can lay sod or plant again. Ask for it to be quoted together with the removal." },
  ]}
  detailTitle="What Affects the Price of a Tree Removal"
  detail={`<p>Every tree is different, so a reliable price comes from someone who has seen or at least reviewed photos of the job. The usual factors are:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Size and condition.</strong> Height, trunk width and whether the wood is sound or rotten.</li>
  <li><strong>Where it stands.</strong> Distance to the house, pool, fence and power lines, and how much room there is to work.</li>
  <li><strong>Access.</strong> Whether a truck and equipment can reach the tree, or the pieces have to be carried out.</li>
  <li><strong>Equipment.</strong> A crane or lift costs more than a climbing crew, but is sometimes the safest way.</li>
  <li><strong>Stump and cleanup.</strong> Whether grinding and hauling away the wood are in the price.</li>
  <li><strong>Permits.</strong> Some cities charge a fee and need time; see our <a href="/tree-removal-permits/">permit guide</a>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Be careful with prices that seem too good to be true. A crew without proper insurance or equipment can turn a cheap removal into an expensive repair, so ask for proof of license and insurance first.</p>
<h3>Signs a tree should come down</h3>
<ul>
  <li>The canopy is brown or bare out of season, or large limbs are dead.</li>
  <li>The tree has started to lean, or the soil at the base is lifting.</li>
  <li>The trunk has deep cracks, splits or mushrooms growing at the base.</li>
  <li>Roots are damaging the foundation, pool, pipes or pavement.</li>
</ul>
<p>Not sure it has to go? A <a href="/certified-arborist/">tree assessment from a local arborist</a> can tell you, and gives you the paperwork if a permit or the state exemption applies.</p>`}
  faqs={faqs}
  image="crane-lift"
  introImage="trunk-cutting"
  introTitle="Safe Tree Removal, Quoted Locally"
  extras={extras}
/>
